**Defect: out-of-bounds `std::vector` access in `compute(const gsMapData<T>&, gsFuncData<T>&)`**

```cpp
compute(in.points, out);

const int parDim = domainDim();
const int nGrads = out.values[1].rows() / parDim;   // evaluated unconditionally

if (out.flags & NEED_DERIV)
{
    ...
}
```

The first `compute(in.points, out)` call runs `evalAllDers_into(in, out.maxDeriv(), out.values)`, which does `result.resize(n+1)`. The size of `out.values` therefore depends on the requested flags:

- With only `NEED_VALUE` (or `NEED_ACTIVE`, etc.), `maxDeriv()` is 0 and `out.values` has exactly one element.
- `out.values[1]` then indexes past the end of the vector. `operator[]` does no bounds check, so `.rows()` reads arbitrary heap memory. This is undefined behavior.
- The garbage value is then divided by `parDim`. If a derived class leaves `domainDim()` at 0, this is also a division by zero.

The only code that uses `nGrads` is inside the `if (out.flags & NEED_DERIV)` block, which is the only case where `values[1]` is guaranteed to exist. Hoisting the computation above that guard adds nothing and makes a memory-unsafe read reachable on the common "values only" path. This is a memory-safety bug, not a style issue.

**Fix:** move both declarations inside the guard:
```cpp
if (out.flags & NEED_DERIV)
{
    const index_t parDim = domainDim();
    const index_t nGrads = out.values[1].rows() / parDim;
    for (index_t p = 0; p != in.points.cols(); ++p) { ... }
}
```

**Secondary concern (non-blocking):** with `SAME_ELEMENT`, `active_into(in.col(0), ...)` assumes `in` has at least one column. Calling it with an empty point set would be another out-of-bounds read. Consider adding a `GISMO_ASSERT(in.cols() > 0, ...)`.

The rest of the patch looks fine: the default stubs, the `eval`/`deriv`/`deriv2` wrappers, and `evalAllDers_into` dispatch. The Eigen product `grads = fform * grads` is alias-safe, because products evaluate into a temporary by default. The unconditional `values[1]` access is enough on its own to reject.

