votes.reasoning:
The patch introduces a null-pointer dereference when reading corner values from XML files that lack a `component` attribute.

**The defect:** the new read path dereferences the attribute without checking it exists:

```cpp
const int cIndex = atoi(child->first_attribute("component")->value());
```

`first_attribute()` returns `NULL` when the attribute is missing, so `->value()` crashes on any `<cv>` node without `component`.

**Why this will happen in practice:**

1. **Old files lack the attribute.** Before this patch, `put()` never wrote `component` on `<cv>` nodes. Every corner value saved by the previous version will crash the new reader.
2. **Hand-written files lack it too.** Hand-written XML inputs and existing test fixtures with `<cv unknown=".." patch=".." corner="..">` will also crash.
3. **It contradicts the convention a few lines above.** The `<bc>` loop treats `component` as optional and defaults to `-1`:
   ```cpp
   const gsXmlAttribute * comp = child->first_attribute("component");
   int cIndex = -1;
   if (NULL != comp)
       cIndex = atoi( comp->value() );
   ```
   Here `component` is an extra, semantically optional field, so it should get the same treatment.

**Suggested fix:**

```cpp
const gsXmlAttribute * comp = child->first_attribute("component");
const int cIndex = (NULL != comp) ? atoi(comp->value()) : -1;
const int cornIndex = atoi(child->first_attribute("corner")->value());
```

**What is correct:** the rest of the patch is sound. Splitting the old `cIndex` (which actually held the corner) into `cornIndex` and a real component index fixes a confusing name. The `addCornerValue(cornIndex, val, pIndex, uIndex, cIndex)` argument order matches `(corner, value, patch, unknown, component)`. The writer now emits `component`, so new files round-trip correctly. The only problem is that the reader no longer accepts files written before this change, and fixing the optional-attribute handling would make the patch approvable.

